5.1 The idea
Ask: what would each county’s rate be if it had the age structure of a common standard population? Apply the county’s own stratum-specific rates \(r_{ij}\) to a fixed set of standard weights \(w_j\):
\[
\text{DSR}_i = \sum_j r_{ij}\, w_j, \qquad \sum_j w_j = 1 .
\]
The standard can be the study population (here, Pennsylvania), an external one (the US 2000 standard population is common in US reporting), or any other fixed reference. Its choice changes the level of the DSRs; it rarely changes their ordering much.


Code
std &lt;- d |&gt;
  group_by(age) |&gt;
  summarise(w = sum(population)) |&gt;
  mutate(w = w / sum(w))                        # PA age structure

cells &lt;- d |&gt;
  group_by(county, age) |&gt;
  summarise(cases = sum(cases), pop = sum(population), .groups = "drop") |&gt;
  mutate(r = cases / pop)                       # county x age rates

dsr &lt;- cells |&gt;
  left_join(std, by = "age") |&gt;
  group_by(county) |&gt;
  summarise(dsr = 1e5 * sum(r * w))

county &lt;- county |&gt; left_join(dsr, by = "county")


We standardized by age only, four strata. With all 16 strata, many cells would hold one or two people of “other” race aged 70+ in a rural county.
